        "text": "{\"scores\":{\"center\":4,\"craft\":4,\"facts\":5,\"age_fit\":4,\"continuity\":5,\"delight\":5},\"evidence\":{\"center\":\"Both boys drive most chapters. Phoenix presses the glowing brick, pulls the quack-clack duck, tugs Ole's sleeve and does the shake test ('He pulled. It held. He shook.'). Cruz pitches 'Super Awesome Toy Company', wiggles the wobbly brick and suggests the tubes. Scoring the worse-covered child, Cruz has no individual action or line in Chapter 5 (only Phoenix's 'Like a LEGO city!') and appears only in group actions in Chapters 7 and 8 ('The three boys dumped out…', 'The boys ran from model to model'), so this is about all-but-one-to-two chapters.\",\"craft\":\"Strong frame: a cold open on a real rainy-Saturday tower, a clear glowing-brick device, a setback-and-rebuild middle and a hands-on climax where the tubes click. The ending calls back to the opening ('This time… it didn't glow. It just went… CLICK.'). The late chapters sag, though. Chapter 8 (LEGOLAND) and Chapter 9 (minifigure) are brief stops with no discovery or challenge for the kids, and the time-travel rules shift from living through years with Ole to sudden 'Whoooosh!' jumps.\",\"facts\":\"All facts are accurate and match the True Facts list: 1932 wooden toys, 'leg godt' in 1934, the rebuilt workshop and the 'Only the best is good enough' motto, the 1947 moulding machine and Automatic Binding Bricks, the 28 January 1958 patent, Kjeld as boy tester and later boss, LEGOLAND in 1968 and the 1978 minifigure. They are woven into action, such as the wobble test that shows why the early bricks failed. Godtfred's boat conversation is well documented, so it needs no hedge.\",\"age_fit\":\"At 1540 words the story is inside the range, and it has short sentences, repetition and plenty of sound words for a 4-year-old, with 'patent' and 'system' hooks for the 7-year-old. However, band A calls for no peril, and Chapter 3's night fire ('Orange flames danced in the dark… they ran outside… burned away') is real peril for a 4-year-old. It is resolved within the chapter, but it is heavier than the band allows.\",\"continuity\":\"This is the first story in the series with no bible, and it is internally consistent. It sets up a good recurring device (the glowing red brick) and a catchphrase ('PLAY WELL!').\",\"delight\":\"Very readable aloud: 'quack-clack, quack-clack', 'CLUNK. HISSSS. POP!' and 'Plink!' all land well. The 'IT CLICKS!' shout-along works, 'The years flew by like pages in a flip book' is a nice image, and the quiet final 'CLICK. And it held.' is a lovely closing beat. There are no AI tells or planted instructions.\"},\"caps_applied\":[\"none\"],\"disqualified\":false,\"overall\":4.45,\"editor_notes\":[\"Soften Chapter 3 for band A. Keep the true fact that the workshop burned, but move the fire offstage: the boys could arrive the morning after to find Ole in the ashes. This removes the night-time flames and running while keeping the 'we will build again' beat.\",\"Give Chapters 8 and 9 a hands-on beat each. For example, Cruz could spot a tiny LEGO Billund in LEGOLAND and find Ole's workshop, and Phoenix could bend the minifigure's legs to sit it in their car from Chapter 7. As written, both are brief stops where the boys only point and gasp.\",\"Give Cruz his own moment in Chapter 5 and his own action in Chapters 7 and 8 (a question for Godtfred, or the first thing he builds), so both boys are individually active in every chapter rather than sharing group verbs.\"],\"best_moment\":\"He pulled. It held. He shook. It held. He turned it upside down and shook it really hard. It still held!\",\"worst_moment\":\"The boys ran from model to model, pointing and gasping, until their legs were tired.\"}"
